DESCRIPTION

  TLS for lua-http, over the mbedtls already vendored in
  third_party/mbedtls.  Hand-written, not vendored.

  The C half, ltls.c and ltlsengine.c, is deliberately small: random
  bytes, message digests, and a non-blocking TLS engine.  The engine
  owns no event loop.  Every call answers either a result or one of the
  strings "want_read" and "want_write", and whoever owns the poll loop,
  which is third_party/cqueues, waits and calls again.

  The Lua half, under .lua/openssl/, is the luaossl-shaped API that
  lua-http requires: openssl.ssl, openssl.ssl.context, openssl.pkey,
  openssl.x509, openssl.x509.verify_param, openssl.x509.name,
  openssl.x509.altname, openssl.rand, openssl.digest and
  openssl.bignum.  A context is a plain record of what the caller asked
  for, and cqueues/socket/tls.lua turns it into mbedtls configuration
  when a handshake actually starts.

HOW THE OPENSSL CALLS MAP

  setOptions becomes a version floor, setGroups and setEphemeralKey
  become a curve list, setAlpnProtos and getAlpnSelected become mbedtls'
  ALPN pair, setHostName and the verify parameter's setHost become
  mbedtls_ssl_set_hostname, which also drives SNI, setVerify becomes
  conf_authmode, and setStore with addDefaults becomes conf_ca_chain over
  GetSslRoots().

  setCipherList is translated rather than ignored.  jart's mbedtls fork
  names its ciphersuites with OpenSSL spellings, so lua-http's strings
  mostly match outright; two spellings differ mechanically and are
  normalised in cqueues/socket/tls.lua.  Concrete names that do not
  resolve are dropped, OpenSSL's grouping and exclusion atoms such as
  HIGH and !aNULL are ignored because mbedtls has no equivalent, and a
  list that resolves to nothing at all raises rather than quietly
  falling back to mbedtls' defaults.

LIMITATIONS

  TLS 1.2 is the ceiling, because this mbedtls has 1.3 compiled out.

  Certificate generation is not implemented, so lua-http's convenience
  path for inventing a self-signed server certificate does not work.  A
  TLS server needs a certificate supplied.

  setAlpnSelect cannot be honoured as a callback, since mbedtls selects
  internally from the configured list.  Configuring the list gets the
  same outcome for every use lua-http makes of it.

TWO THINGS WORTH KNOWING

  mbedtls' contexts want stronger alignment than the body of a Lua
  userdata gives, so the engine lives in malloc'd memory with a pointer
  in the userdata.  Holding the config inline segfaults.

  A curve id the library does not have fails the handshake with
  BAD_CONFIG rather than being ignored, so the curve list is filtered
  through mbedtls_ecp_curve_info_from_grp_id.  lua-http asks for P-521,
  which this mbedtls does not have.
